ca.append(M(A,19,'Electric-field components','D.2','Two charges $+Q$ and $-Q$ are separated horizontally. Point P is distance $r$ from each charge, with the lines to P making $60^\\circ$ to the horizontal. Let $E_0=kQ/r^2$. What is the resultant electric field at P?', ['$E_0$ to the right','$2E_0$ to the right','$\\sqrt3E_0$ upward','Zero'],'At P the field from $+Q$ is upward-right and the field toward $-Q$ is downward-right. The vertical components cancel. The horizontal components add to $2E_0\\cos60^\\circ=E_0$ right. Electric field is a vector.','6.pdf',9,'19','Dipole symmetry and component addition',dipole))
ca.append(M(A,20,'A velocity selector and a magnetic analyser','D.3','A particle of mass $m$ and positive charge $q$ passes undeflected through crossed fields, then enters a region containing only the same magnetic field, following radius $r$. A second particle has mass $2m$ and charge $q/2$. It also passes undeflected through the selector. What is its analyser radius?', ['$4r$','$2r$','$r$','$r/4$'],'Undeflected motion requires $qE=qvB$, so both selected particles have $v=E/B$, independent of mass and charge. In the magnetic-only region $r=mv/(qB)$. The second ratio is $(2m)/(q/2)$ divided by $m/q$, giving $4r$.','6.pdf',10,'20','Crossed-field selection followed by circular motion'))
ca.append(M(A,21,'Testing the nuclear model','E.1','A very thin metal foil produces a small number of large-angle alpha-particle deflections. Which observation most directly supports concentration of positive charge in a very small nucleus?', ['A tiny fraction of incident particles reverse direction, while most pass nearly straight through.','Every incident particle loses exactly the same fraction of its energy.','All incident particles turn back from the foil surface.','The scattered particles have a continuous range of negative charges.'],'Rare large-angle/backward deflections require a highly concentrated repulsive region. The near-straight passage of most particles implies that this region occupies a small fraction of atomic volume. Uniform positive charge cannot readily account for the rare strong deflections.','6.pdf',10,'21','Rutherford evidence and model discrimination'))
ca.append(M(A,22,'Successive nuclear transformations','E.3','A uranium nucleus ${}^{238}_{92}\\mathrm U$ undergoes alpha decay. Its daughter subsequently undergoes beta-minus decay. What are the final nucleon number and proton number?', ['$234$ and $91$','$234$ and $89$','$238$ and $93$','$236$ and $90$'],'Alpha decay reduces nucleon number by $4$ and proton number by $2$, yielding $A=234,Z=90$. Beta-minus changes a neutron into a proton, leaving $A$ unchanged and increasing $Z$ to $91$. The emitted electron is not a pre-existing orbital electron.','6.pdf',11,'22','Conservation of nucleon number and charge'))
