cb.append(M(B,17,'Equal areas in an elliptical orbit','D.1','At perihelion P a planet is $0.60a$ from its star; at aphelion A it is $1.40a$ away. Its velocity is perpendicular to the star-planet line at both points. What is $v_P/v_A$?', ['$7/3$','$49/9$','$3/7$','$\\sqrt{7/3}$'],"For equal small time intervals, the swept triangular areas are $\\tfrac12r_Pv_P\\Delta t$ and $\\tfrac12r_Av_A\\Delta t$. Kepler's equal-area law gives $r_Pv_P=r_Av_A$, so $v_P/v_A=1.40/0.60=7/3$. This concerns speed, not orbital period.",'a.pdf',8,'17','Equal swept areas rather than equal arc lengths',orbit))
cb.append(M(B,18,'Stability at a zero-field point','D.1','Two isolated fixed masses $M$ and $4M$ are separated by $R$. Their gravitational field is zero at P, between them and $R/3$ from $M$. A small test body at P is displaced slightly toward $4M$ and released. What is its initial acceleration and what does this imply about equilibrium along the line?', ['Toward $4M$; unstable','Toward $M$; stable','Toward $4M$; stable','Zero; neutral'],"Moving toward $4M$ strengthens that mass's attraction and weakens the attraction toward $M$. The resultant therefore points in the displacement direction, toward $4M$, so the body moves farther from P. The equilibrium is unstable along this line.",'a.pdf',8,'18','Field cancellation and inverse-square reasoning',svg(circle(100,140,25,'M')+circle(500,140,40,'4M')+line(125,140,460,140,True)+circle(233,140,4)+text(233,115,'P',15,'middle')+arrow(250,100,320,100,'small displacement')+text(170,190,'R/3',14,'middle'),600,230)))
cb.append(M(B,19,'Order of electrostatic operations','D.2','A negative rod is brought near an initially neutral metal sphere without touching it. The sphere is grounded. In procedure X the ground is removed before the rod. In procedure Y the rod is removed while the ground remains, and the ground is removed last. What are the final sphere charges?', ['X positive; Y neutral','X negative; Y neutral','X positive; Y positive','X neutral; Y negative'],'The negative rod repels electrons from the sphere into Earth. Removing the ground first traps an electron deficit, so X is positive after the rod is removed. If the rod is removed while still grounded, electrons return until the sphere is neutral; Y then remains neutral. Order is essential.','a.pdf',9,'19','Charging by induction and grounding order'))
cb.append(M(B,20,'Magnetic force near a wire','D.3','A long vertical wire carries current upward. A negatively charged particle lies to its right and moves horizontally toward the wire. What is the magnetic-force direction at that instant?', ['Upward, parallel to the current','Downward, opposite to the current','Into the page','Away from the wire'],'The right-hand grip rule gives a magnetic field into the page to the right of the wire. For positive charge, leftward velocity crossed with an into-page field gives a downward force. The negative charge reverses this to upward, parallel to the wire current.','a.pdf',9,'20','Combine a wire field with the charge sign',svg(line(190,40,190,240,width=4)+arrow(190,200,190,70,'current',125,95)+circle(440,140,17,'-')+arrow(415,140,285,140,'velocity',350,115)+text(440,210,'negative particle',14,'middle'),600,280)))
levels=svg(line(90,220,480,220)+line(90,150,480,150)+line(90,45,480,45)+text(505,225,'0 eV',15)+text(505,155,'2.0 eV',15)+text(505,50,'5.0 eV',15)+text(300,270,'three allowed levels',15,'middle'),600,300)
cb.append(M(B,21,'Population and absorption lines','E.1','An atom has the three energy levels shown. A gas is illuminated by photons of energy $3.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}$. Which change can make it absorb these photons, assuming the transitions between the shown levels are allowed?', ['Populate the $2.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}$ level before illumination.','Put every atom in the ground state.','Lower the photon intensity while keeping all atoms in the ground state.','Replace each photon by two separate $1.5\\,\\mathrm{eV}$ photons and assume single-photon absorption.'],'A $3.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}$ photon matches the $5.0-2.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}$ gap. The lower state of that transition must be populated. From the ground state the available gaps are $2.0$ and $5.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}$, so $3.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}$ is not absorbed in this three-level model. Intensity alone does not change photon energy.','a.pdf',10,'21','Discrete transitions and absorption-state population',levels))
