da.append(W(A,'1B',2,'Density from a force-depth gradient','A.2',r'A force sensor suspends a uniform vertical object partly in a liquid. The cross-sectional area is $(4.00\pm0.08)\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^2}$. Immersion depth $h$ remains below the object\'s total length. No part touches the vessel, surface-tension effects are negligible and the object remains stationary. The measured upward sensor force is $F$. A fit to the laboratory data gives\n\n$$F=(2.35\pm0.02)-(3.90\pm0.15)h,$$\n\nwith $F$ in newtons and $h$ in metres. Use $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$.\n\n| $h$/m | 0.00 | 0.03 | 0.06 | 0.09 | 0.12 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| $F$/N | 2.35 | 2.23 | 2.12 | 2.00 | 1.88 |',[
('a',r'Identify the forces on the object and derive $F=W-\rho gAh$.',2),
('b',r'Use the gradient to determine the liquid density.',3),
('c',r'Determine the maximum uncertainty in that density. Neglect uncertainty in $g$.',2),
('d',r'Would a constant $+0.10\,\mathrm N$ force-sensor zero error change the density obtained by this method? Explain.',1),
('e',r'Explain why the straight-line model must stop applying once the object becomes completely immersed.',2)], [
('a',[('R1',r'Downward weight $W$ is balanced by upward sensor force and upthrust.'),('M1',r'The displaced liquid volume is $Ah$, so upthrust is $\rho gAh$ and $F+\rho gAh=W$.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'The gradient is $-\rho gA$, with magnitude $3.90\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$.'),('M1',r'$\rho=3.90/(9.81\times4.00\times10^{-4})$.'),('A1',r'$\rho=994\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ to three significant figures.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'$\delta\rho/\rho=0.15/3.90+0.08/4.00=0.05846$.'),('A1',r'$\delta\rho\approx58\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$; accept $(990\pm60)\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ or consistent rounding.')]),
('d',[('R1',r'No. A constant force offset changes the intercept, but not the force-depth gradient, provided it is constant throughout the measurements.')]),
('e',[('R1',r'Once fully immersed, the displaced volume stops increasing when the object is lowered farther.'),('R1',r'Upthrust and sensor force then remain approximately constant for an incompressible liquid; extending the negative-gradient line would be physically wrong.')])], 'y.pdf',1,'1','Force-sensor buoyancy graph and inference of density',buoy))
rho=A.check('A2B liquid density',3.9/(9.81*4e-4),993.883792049,1e-8);A.check('A2B density uncertainty',rho*(.15/3.9+.08/4),58.1039755352,1e-7)

