db.append(W(B,'1B',2,'Testing a repeated-energy-loss model','A.3',r'A ball bounces vertically on a fixed floor. Air resistance between impacts is negligible. The recorded maximum heights are shown. The measurement uncertainty in every height is $0.005\,\mathrm m$. A student proposes that the same fraction of mechanical energy is retained at each impact.\n\n| Bounce number | 0: release | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Maximum height / m | 0.800 | 0.512 | 0.328 | 0.210 | 0.134 |',[
('a',r'Use the release and first rebound to find the fraction of mechanical energy lost at the first impact.',2),
('b',r'Find the ratio of the speed immediately after this impact to the speed immediately before it.',2),
('c',r'Using the constant-retention model, predict the fifth rebound height from the release height.',2),
('d',r'The measured fifth height is $(0.040\pm0.005)\,\mathrm m$. A propagated model prediction is $(0.086\pm0.007)\,\mathrm m$. Decide whether these data support the model, using the uncertainties.',2),
('e',r'A common calibration error makes every measured height $10\%$ too large. Explain its effect on the energy-retention fraction inferred from height ratios.',2)], [
('a',[('M1',r'Energy at a turning point is $mgh$. The retained fraction is $0.512/0.800=0.640$.'),('A1',r'The fraction lost is $1-0.640=0.360$, or $36.0\%$.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'Kinetic energy is proportional to speed squared; use $v_{\rm after}/v_{\rm before}=\sqrt{0.640}$.'),('A1',r'The speed ratio is $0.800$.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'The fifth rebound follows five impacts, so $h_5=0.800(0.640)^5$.'),('A1',r'$h_5=0.0859\,\mathrm m$, approximately $0.086\,\mathrm m$.')]),
('d',[('R1',r'The measured interval $[0.035,0.045]\,\mathrm m$ and predicted interval $[0.079,0.093]\,\mathrm m$ do not overlap.'),('R1',r'The discrepancy is larger than the stated combined bounds, so the later result does not support constant retention over all five impacts. It does not identify a unique cause of failure.')]),
('e',[('M1',r'Each ratio becomes $(1.10h_{n+1})/(1.10h_n)$.'),('R1',r'The common factor cancels, so the inferred retention fraction is unchanged. Absolute heights and energy estimates would still be biased.')])], 'v.pdf',8,'3','A declining-height data set and tests of a repeated fractional-retention model',bounces))
B.check('B2B retention',.512/.8,.64);B.check('B2B speed ratio',math.sqrt(.64),.8);B.check('B2B fifth height',.8*.64**5,.08589934592)

# SL SET A PAPER 2: six compulsory structured questions, 50 marks.
pa.append(W(A,'2',1,'Coupled carts and a buffer spring','A.2',r'A $1.20\,\mathrm{kg}$ cart moves at $3.00\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and couples to a stationary $0.800\,\mathrm{kg}$ cart on a horizontal track. External impulse during the $0.0500\,\mathrm s$ coupling is negligible. The coupled carts then compress an initially unstretched ideal spring of constant $180\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$. Resistance after the collision is negligible.',[
('a',r'Calculate the common velocity immediately after coupling.',2),('b',r'Calculate the average force exerted on the initially stationary cart during coupling.',2),('c',r'Determine the fraction of the initial total kinetic energy dissipated during coupling.',2),('d',r'Calculate the maximum spring compression.',2)], [
('a',[('M1',r'$1.20(3.00)=(1.20+0.800)v$ by momentum conservation.'),('A1',r'$v=1.80\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ in the initial direction.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'$F_{\rm av}=\Delta p/\Delta t=0.800(1.80)/0.0500$.'),('A1',r'$F_{\rm av}=28.8\,\mathrm N$ in the initial moving cart\'s direction.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'$K_i=\tfrac12(1.20)(3.00)^2=5.40\,\mathrm J$ and $K_f=\tfrac12(2.00)(1.80)^2=3.24\,\mathrm J$.'),('A1',r'The dissipated fraction is $(5.40-3.24)/5.40=0.400$.')]),
('d',[('M1',r'After coupling, $\tfrac12kx^2=3.24\,\mathrm J$. Do not reuse the pre-collision kinetic energy.'),('A1',r'$x=\sqrt{2(3.24)/180}=0.190\,\mathrm m$.')])], 'q.pdf',1,'1','Spring-associated cart collision, changed to coupling followed by energy storage',svg(rect(70,110,120,50,'1.20 kg')+arrow(70,80,190,80,'3.00 m/s')+rect(280,110,120,50,'0.800 kg')+line(40,165,560,165)+line(500,75,500,160,width=4)+'<polyline points="410,130 420,130 430,115 440,145 450,115 460,145 470,130 500,130" fill="none" stroke="#202020" stroke-width="2"/>'+text(455,60,'buffer spring',14,'middle'),600,220)))
A.check('AP2 cart velocity',1.2*3/2,1.8);A.check('AP2 collision force',.8*1.8/.05,28.8);A.check('AP2 energy loss fraction',(5.4-3.24)/5.4,.4);A.check('AP2 compression',math.sqrt(2*3.24/180),.1897366596101,1e-10)

pa.append(W(A,'2',2,'Insulating a cold-room wall','B.1',r'A wall of area $12.0\,\mathrm{m^2}$, thickness $0.200\,\mathrm m$ and thermal conductivity $0.800\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ separates air at $22.0^\circ\mathrm C$ from air at $2.0^\circ\mathrm C$. Treat the wall surfaces as being at these temperatures. An insulating layer of thickness $0.0500\,\mathrm m$ and conductivity $0.0400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ is added to the cold side. Assume steady one-dimensional conduction and no other losses. The heater converts $80.0\%$ of electrical input into useful room heating.',[
('a',r'Describe conduction in the solid wall at the particle level.',2),('b',r'Calculate the heat-transfer rate through the bare wall.',2),('c',r'Calculate the heat-transfer rate after insulation is added.',2),('d',r'Calculate the wall-insulation interface temperature.',1),('e',r'Calculate the electrical energy saved in $10.0\,\mathrm h$, in $\mathrm{kWh}$.',1)], [
('a',[('R1',r'Particles at the hot side have greater mean random vibrational energy and transfer energy to neighbouring particles through interactions.'),('R1',r'There is net energy transfer down the temperature gradient without bulk motion of the solid; free electrons can also carry energy in a conducting material.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'$P=kA\Delta T/L=0.800(12.0)(20.0)/0.200$.'),('A1',r'$P=960\,\mathrm W$.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'Add series thermal resistances: $P=A\Delta T/(L_1/k_1+L_2/k_2)$.'),('A1',r'$P=12.0(20.0)/(0.200/0.800+0.0500/0.0400)=160\,\mathrm W$.')]),
('d',[('A1',r'The wall drop is $PL_1/(k_1A)=3.33\,\mathrm K$, so the interface is $22.0-3.33=18.7^\circ\mathrm C$.')]),
('e',[('A1',r'Input power saved is $(960-160)/0.800=1000\,\mathrm W=1.00\,\mathrm{kW}$, so energy saved is $10.0\,\mathrm{kWh}$.')])], 'q.pdf',3,'2','Wall conduction with new insulation and an electrical-energy comparison',svg(rect(160,70,160,170,'wall')+rect(320,70,55,170)+text(348,55,'insulation',14,'middle')+text(75,150,'22.0 C',16)+text(435,150,'2.0 C',16)+arrow(205,275,375,275,'heat flow')+text(240,260,'0.200 m',14,'middle')+text(348,260,'0.0500 m',13,'middle'),600,310)))
A.check('AP2 bare wall',.8*12*20/.2,960);A.check('AP2 insulated wall',12*20/(.2/.8+.05/.04),160);A.check('AP2 interface',22-160*.2/(.8*12),18.6666666667,1e-9)

pa.append(W(A,'2',3,'Interference positions and a new medium','C.3',r'Coherent light of wavelength $600\,\mathrm{nm}$ in air illuminates two narrow slits separated by $0.300\,\mathrm{mm}$. A screen is $2.50\,\mathrm m$ from the slits. Use the small-angle approximation. The light frequency remains fixed throughout.',[
('a',r'Explain why a fixed phase relationship between the slits is required for a stable fringe pattern.',2),('b',r'Calculate the separation of adjacent bright fringes.',2),('c',r'Calculate the distance between the first dark fringe to the left of the central maximum and the second dark fringe to its right.',2),('d',r'The entire slit-screen region is filled with a transparent medium of refractive index $1.33$. Calculate the new bright-fringe separation and explain the change.',2)], [
('a',[('R1',r'A stable phase difference lets the same positions retain constructive or destructive interference.'),('R1',r'If the phase difference changes randomly, bright and dark positions move rapidly and the time-averaged pattern washes out.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'$s=\lambda D/d=(600\times10^{-9})(2.50)/(0.300\times10^{-3})$.'),('A1',r'$s=5.00\,\mathrm{mm}$.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'Dark positions are at half-integer fringe spacings. The requested positions are $-0.5s$ and $+1.5s$.'),('A1',r'The separation is $2s=10.0\,\mathrm{mm}$.')]),
('d',[('M1',r'Frequency is unchanged while speed and wavelength are reduced by factor $1.33$, so fringe spacing is also divided by $1.33$.'),('A1',r'$s_{\rm new}=5.00/1.33=3.76\,\mathrm{mm}$.')])], 'q.pdf',9,'4','Coherence and positions of destructive interference, with a medium change',svg(line(125,30,125,115)+line(125,125,125,175)+line(125,185,125,270)+line(510,30,510,270)+line(125,150,510,150,True)+line(125,120,510,195)+line(125,180,510,195)+text(515,155,'centre',13)+text(300,285,'D = 2.50 m',15,'middle')+text(65,145,'two slits',13,'middle'),600,310)))
A.check('AP2 fringe spacing',600e-9*2.5/.0003,.005);A.check('AP2 water fringe spacing',.005/1.33,.00375939849624,1e-12)

pa.append(W(A,'2',4,'Does a proton leave the deflector?','D.2',r'A proton enters midway between horizontal parallel plates with horizontal speed $1.00\times10^5\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Plate separation is $0.0400\,\mathrm m$, plate length $0.0300\,\mathrm m$ and potential difference $240\,\mathrm V$. The upper plate is negative. Neglect gravity and edge effects. Use proton charge $1.60\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$ and mass $1.67\times10^{-27}\,\mathrm{kg}$.',[
('a',r'Calculate the electric-field strength and the force on the proton, including direction.',2),('b',r'Calculate the proton acceleration.',2),('c',r'Assuming it could travel the full plate length, calculate its vertical displacement by the exit plane.',2),('d',r'Decide whether the proton actually reaches that plane while remaining between the plates. Justify your conclusion.',2)], [
('a',[('A1',r'$E=V/d=240/0.0400=6.00\times10^3\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}$, upward toward the negative plate.'),('A1',r'$F=qE=9.60\times10^{-16}\,\mathrm N$, upward.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'$a=F/m=9.60\times10^{-16}/(1.67\times10^{-27})$.'),('A1',r'$a=5.75\times10^{11}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, upward.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'Horizontal speed is unchanged, so $t=L/v_x=3.00\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm s$.'),('A1',r'$y=\tfrac12at^2=0.0259\,\mathrm m$.')]),
('d',[('R1',r'The available upward clearance from the entry midpoint is only $0.0200\,\mathrm m$, less than the projected displacement.'),('R1',r'The proton therefore hits the upper plate before the exit plane; the unimpeded trajectory used in (c) cannot describe motion beyond contact. Accept the equivalent calculation $x_{\rm hit}=0.0264\,\mathrm m<0.0300\,\mathrm m$.')])], 'q.pdf',7,'3','Charged-particle motion in a uniform plate field, changed to transverse entry and collision test',svg(line(130,60,500,60,width=4)+line(130,240,500,240,width=4)+text(315,40,'negative plate',15,'middle')+text(315,270,'positive plate',15,'middle')+circle(140,150,9,'+')+arrow(40,150,120,150,'entry')+arrow(330,210,330,95,'E',365,150)+text(535,160,'0.0400 m',14,'middle'),640,300)))
acc=A.check('AP2 proton acceleration',1.60e-19*6000/1.67e-27,5.74850299401e11,1);A.check('AP2 projected deflection',.5*acc*(.03/1e5)**2,.0258682634731,1e-11)

decay=plot([(0,270),(10,150),(20,90),(30,60),(40,45)],(0,40),(0,300),'time / min','expected gross count rate / s-1',(0,10,20,30,40),(0,30,90,150,270))
pa.append(W(A,'2',5,'An activated sample above background','E.3',r'An activated sample undergoes beta-minus decay, forming a stable daughter. Its initial parent-nucleus count is $8.00\times10^9$. A detector has a constant background rate $30.0\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Its expected gross count rates are $270\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at $t=0$ and $90.0\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at $t=20.0\,\mathrm{min}$. Detector efficiency remains constant. The illustrative curve shows expected rates rather than individual random measurements.',[
('a',r'For a parent ${}^{A}_{Z}X$, write the beta-minus decay equation with a daughter Y and all emitted particles.',2),('b',r'Determine the sample half-life.',2),('c',r'Predict the gross count rate at $30.0\,\mathrm{min}$.',2),('d',r'Determine the number of parent nuclei remaining at $30.0\,\mathrm{min}$.',1),('e',r'Explain why a short measured count rate need not lie exactly on the curve and can occasionally be below the expected background rate.',2)], [
('a',[('A1',r'The daughter has nucleon number $A$ and proton number $Z+1$.'),('A1',r'${}^{A}_{Z}X\to{}^{A}_{Z+1}Y+{}^{0}_{-1}e+\bar\nu_e$. An electron antineutrino accompanies the beta-minus electron.')]),
('b',[('M1',r'Subtract background: source rates are $240$ and $60.0\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, a reduction by $4$.'),('A1',r'This is two half-lives in $20.0\,\mathrm{min}$, so $T_{1/2}=10.0\,\mathrm{min}$.')]),
('c',[('M1',r'After three half-lives the source rate is $240/8=30.0\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$.'),('A1',r'Add background to obtain $60.0\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ gross.')]),
('d',[('A1',r'$N=8.00\times10^9/8=1.00\times10^9$ parent nuclei.')]),
('e',[('R1',r'Radioactive detection and background events are random, so finite-interval counts fluctuate around their expected values.'),('R1',r'The expected background is a mean, not a strict lower bound on a measured short-interval count rate; sufficiently long averages approach the expected curve. No negative physical source rate is implied.')])], '6.pdf',11,'23','Background-corrected half-life inference and random-count interpretation',decay))
